<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otomi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Otomí people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Otomi are an indigenous people of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, living mainly in the states of Hidalgo, México and Querétaro and speaking Otomian languages of the Oto-Manguean family. Before the Spanish conquest they lived both inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> and in independent lordships such as Metztitlan, and the Aztecs named an elite warrior rank after them. Today several hundred thousand people speak Otomi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otomi.jpg" alt="Otomi" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maunus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrieros_acazulco.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Otomi are linguistically related to the other Otomanguean-speaking peoples, whose ancestors have occupied the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt for several thousand years. The name Otomi is of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> origin, from otómitl, meaning one who walks with arrows, and is not native to the people. They call themselves by several names in their own languages, among them ñätho in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluca-valley">Toluca Valley</a> and hñähñu in the Mezquital Valley. Linguistic evidence suggests that the western branch to which the Otomi belong reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> around the fourth millennium BCE, probably from the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Some historians believe that the Otomi were the first inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanec</a> expelled them in 1418. After the fall of Teotihuacan, where they were one of several peoples, Nahuatl-speaking groups began to displace them eastward. Around 1100 CE Otomi speakers formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xaltocan">Xaltocan</a>, which demanded tribute from nearby communities until it was subjugated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and their allies conquered the Otomi kingdom in the 14th century, and the Otomi then paid tribute to the Aztec Triple Alliance.</p><p>Many Otomi in Tlaxcala joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> against the Alliance, and those of Ixtenco went on to found the city of Querétaro. The Otomi of the Mezquital Valley resisted the Spanish with raids until the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines were opened. By the 1530s all the Otomi communities of the Mezquital Valley and the Barranca de Meztitlán had been divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a>. The later Indian republics gave their communities some autonomy, which helped them keep their language, but land was lost throughout the colonial period and epidemics wiped out numerous communities between the sixteenth and eighteenth centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Otomi traditionally lived on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash, and the maguey was also important, giving pulque, a fermented drink, and fibre. Before the conquest their religion was polytheist and close to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a>, and many Otomi still practise shamanism and pre-Hispanic beliefs. They were also metalworkers who traded ornaments and weapons with other peoples, the Aztec Triple Alliance among them.</p><p>In 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s National Institute of Indigenous Peoples counted 667,038 Otomi, the fifth largest Indigenous people in the country, mostly in Hidalgo, Mexico and Querétaro, and little more than half of them spoke Otomi. The Otomi languages belong to the Oto-Pamean branch of the Oto-Manguean family and are closely related to Mazahua. Sources count between four and nine of them, and some are not mutually intelligible.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otomi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
